# The watch list: an incident is ours if it touches one of these packages.
# Read by bin/mu-incidents. One package per line; '#' starts a comment.
#
# Names are written as the squad's SMELT list has them, in binary-package terms
# ("libguestfs0"). mu-incidents drops a trailing soname digit from both sides
# before comparing, so they also match the source-package names the dashboard
# and SMELT report ("libguestfs").
#
# kernel-source is the one most likely to surprise you: a kernel incident is a
# virtualization incident, because the hypervisor rides on it. It also produces
# the widest fan-out of red boxes when it breaks.
#
# The list is packages, not products. The same package appears across SLE 12,
# 15, 16 and SL Micro.
#
# Something virtualization-shaped that is not on this list is a question for the
# team channel, not a silent yes or no. `mu-incidents --near` shows those.
#
# Source: "New MU VT incident test process since 2023", qasle space, What/Duties,
# last updated 2025-02-17.
# https://confluence.suse.com/spaces/qasle/pages/1278050323

xen
qemu
libvirt
kvm
kernel-source
libguestfs0
virt-manager
libslirp0
open-vm-tools
